Radia Perlman was born in Portsmouth, Virginia, in 1951. Both of her parents were engineers for the US government. She was the only woman in her high school programming class, and she went on to study mathematics at MIT.

## Teaching toddlers to code

As a student at MIT's LOGO Lab, working under Seymour Papert, she built TORTIS, a version of the LOGO language for very young children. Kids as young as three and a half programmed a robot "turtle" by pressing buttons. She is considered a pioneer of teaching programming to young children.

## Networks that fix themselves

At Digital Equipment Corporation in the 1980s she invented the Spanning Tree Protocol (STP). It lets network switches, connected in any pattern, automatically find a path without data looping forever. Networks could now organize themselves instead of being set up by hand. She also made link-state routing, which the internet uses to choose paths, more robust and easier to manage.

- **2006:** Received the USENIX Lifetime Achievement Award.
- **2010:** Received the ACM SIGCOMM Award.
- **2014:** Inducted into the Internet Hall of Fame.
- **2016:** Inducted into the National Inventors Hall of Fame.
- **2019:** Elected to the National Academy of Engineering.

She later invented TRILL, which fixes some of spanning tree's limits, and she holds more than 100 patents. She has asked not to be called the "Mother of the Internet," saying no single person built it.

